# 🚀 Module 8: Practical Git Exercises

---

## 🔹 Exercise 1: Basic Git Workflow

### 1. Create a new directory and initialize Git

```bash
mkdir git-practice
cd git-practice
git init
```

### 2. Create a README.md file

```bash
echo "# My Git Practice" > README.md
```

### 3. Add and commit the file

```bash
git add README.md
git commit -m "Initial commit with README"
```

### 4. Make changes and commit again

```bash
echo "This is my first Git project" >> README.md
git add README.md
git commit -m "Updated README with description"
```

### 5. View commit history

```bash
git log
```

---

## 🔹 Exercise 2: Branching and Merging

### 1. Create a new branch

```bash
git checkout -b feature
```

### 2. Add new files in feature branch

```bash
echo "Feature work" > feature.txt
git add feature.txt
git commit -m "Added feature file"
```

### 3. Switch back to main branch

```bash
git checkout main
```

### 4. Merge feature branch into main

```bash
git merge feature
```

### 5. Delete the feature branch

```bash
git branch -d feature
```

---

## 🔹 Exercise 3: Conflict Resolution

### 1. Create two branches

```bash
git checkout -b branch1
git checkout main
git checkout -b branch2
```

### 2. Modify same file in both branches

**Branch 1:**

```bash
git checkout branch1
echo "Line from branch1" > conflict.txt
git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "branch1 changes"
```

**Branch 2:**

```bash
git checkout branch2
echo "Line from branch2" > conflict.txt
git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "branch2 changes"
```

### 3. Merge one branch into main

```bash
git checkout main
git merge branch1
```

### 4. Merge second branch (conflict occurs)

```bash
git merge branch2
```

⚠️ You will see a merge conflict.

### 5. Resolve conflict manually

```text
<<<<<<< HEAD
Line from branch1
=======
Line from branch2
>>>>>>> branch2
```

✅ Fix it like:

```text
Line from branch1
Line from branch2
```

```bash
git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "Resolved merge conflict"
```

